 "readme": "# Investigating CTGAN Usage in CTR Prediction Tasks\n\nThis project investigates how **feature selection**, **down-sampling**, and **data permutation** affect the downstream utility of synthetically generated data from **CTGAN** in **highly imbalanced classification tasks**.  \nWe empirically test these transformations on **Huawei's Ad Click-Through Rate (CTR)** dataset using **CatBoost** as the classification model.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 Overview\n\nReal-world classification datasets, such as ad click prediction, often suffer from extreme class imbalance — in this case, only **1.5%** of samples correspond to clicks.  \nWe employ **CTGAN (Conditional GAN for Tabular Data)** to generate synthetic minority samples and evaluate three common data transformations to improve performance:\n\n1. **Feature Selection:** How including additional non-important features impacts CTGAN’s data diversity.  \n2. **Down-Sampling:** Whether adjusting class imbalance before CTGAN training improves synthetic data quality.  \n3. **Data Permutation:** The effect of controlled column-wise shuffling on diversity and model generalization.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ Methodology\n\n**Pipeline Summary**\n1. Clean and preprocess the Huawei CTR dataset (categorical & discrete predictors).  \n2. Train **CTGAN** on training data with different transformations.  \n3. Generate synthetic data (20% of training size).  \n4. Combine real and synthetic data for downstream training using **CatBoost**.  \n5. Evaluate performance using **AUC**, **Precision**, **Recall**, and **F1-score**.\n\n**Main Libraries**\n- `ctgan` for synthetic tabular data generation  \n- `catboost` for classification  \n- `pandas`, `numpy`, `scikit-learn` for data processing and evaluation  \n- `matplotlib` and `seaborn` for visualization\n\n---\n\n## 🧪 Key Findings\n\n| Technique | Observation | Effect |\n|------------|--------------|--------|\n| **Feature Selection** | Removing or heavily filtering features reduced downstream utility | ↓ Performance |\n| **Down-Sampling (to 5%)** | Increased minority proportion in CTGAN training data | **↑ Recall & F1-score (≈300%)** |\n| **Permutation (≤20%)** | Slight regularization effect on AUC; excessive noise degraded results | ↕ Minor Gain |\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Results Summary\n\n- Optimal CTGAN performance when minority class ≈ **5%** of training data.  \n- Down-sampling improved **recall** and **F1-score** by up to **3× baseline**.  \n- Over-permutation (≥50%) degraded model generalization.  \n- Feature pruning led to reduced diversity and weaker synthetic sample utility.\n\n",
